WEBVTT

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:04.190
Hi. How are your outlines coming along?

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.110 
Have you got them done?

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.130
Yes. I have outlined the structure of my presentation

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.100 
Now I am working on the content.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.250
Great. That means you have a good “recipe” done

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.070
What recipe? I’m not cooking

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.210 
I’m writing my presentation

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.250
I know. But I’ve heard an interesting example

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.200
It says preparing for a presentation is like making a cake

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.210
That IS interesting

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.230 
So, what is the “recipe” for a presentation?

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.060
A good recipe is the structure

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.070
plus an outline for each section

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.120 
You need to have the outlines first

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.100 
and then follow the instructions carefully 

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.060
in order to make the cake

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.000
Very true

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.160
Without the outlines

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.150 
the content may end up being vague and unorganized

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.120 
So now you’re writing the content

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.280 
It’s like mixing all the ingredients together in a bowl

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.200 
You’re right

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.190  
That’s the main body of my presentation

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.030 
I need to write down what I want the audience to hear

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.240 
Those are my “ingredients.”

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.140 
When you write down the main points

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.020 
focus on the core message

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.020  
and keep it clear and concise

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.230  
Avoid using jargon that people won’t understand

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.100 
Your audience won’t have time to check the dictionary

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.000 
And avoid abbreviations, acronyms and initials, too

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.040  
Presentations are spoken

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.210 
so choose terms people are very familiar with

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.100 
The “rule of three” is very practical

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.130  
Limit your main points to three

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.050 
and then expand on each of them if necessary

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.000  
Keep them simple and short.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000 
I totally agree

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.060 
People lose interest in long sentences 

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.190 
and information overload

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.050 
Those are all good tips 

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.180 
for getting the main points ready

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.070  
When you’re done with the content

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.160  
it’s time to put the “cake” in the oven to cook

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.250 
So what’s the next step after “baking”?

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.220 
Remove the cake from the oven, and cool it

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.250  
You “cool” your presentation draft

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.290 
when you edit and revise it

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.230 
Right. Editing and revising take time

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.280  
To revise the first draft, read it aloud

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.050  
and listen to the rhythm of the words

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.120  
Ensure the language is appropriate and easy to understand

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.110 
You should probably review it more than once

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.120  
and cut anything that is not relevant to your core message

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.150  
After working on your revision

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.000
you can start rehearsing your talk

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.010  
Wow, sounds perfect

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.220  
I can’t wait to make my very own “cake.”
